<header class="detail-head">
	<h2 class="page-hed">Embed a score</h2>
	<p class="lede">
		A standalone custom element that shows one site's Lighthouse scores, with a
		summary on hover or focus. Drop it on any page — it needs nothing but the
		script and this instance's URL.
	</p>
</header>

<script type="module" src="/js/speedlify-score.js"></script>

<section class="panel">
	<h2>Live examples</h2>
	<p class="muted small">
		Hover or tab to a set of scores to see its summary. Each example is a real
		lookup against this instance. The last one is a site that emits a generator
		tag, so its summary carries a "Built with" row — most sites here emit none,
		and the row is simply absent for those.
	</p>

	{#
		A deliberate spread, not the top of the leaderboard. Three perfect scores
		in a row demonstrate the green state and nothing else — the component's
		whole job is showing where a site actually sits, so at least one example
		has to be a site with problems.

		Picked by walking the ranked list from both ends rather than hardcoding
		URLs, which would rot the moment those sites changed or left the corpus.
	#}
	{% set measured = [] %}
	{% for entry in sites.entries %}
		{% if entry.latest %}{% set measured = (measured.push(entry), measured) %}{% endif %}
	{% endfor %}

	{% set demo = [] %}
	{% set demoUrls = [] %}
	{% if measured.length %}
		{% set demo = (demo.push(measured[0]), demo) %}
		{% set demoUrls = (demoUrls.push(measured[0].url), demoUrls) %}
		{% if measured.length > 2 %}
			{% set mid = measured[(measured.length / 2) | round | int] %}
			{% set demo = (demo.push(mid), demo) %}
			{% set demoUrls = (demoUrls.push(mid.url), demoUrls) %}
		{% endif %}
		{% if measured.length > 1 %}
			{% set last = measured[measured.length - 1] %}
			{% set demo = (demo.push(last), demo) %}
			{% set demoUrls = (demoUrls.push(last.url), demoUrls) %}
		{% endif %}
	{% endif %}

	{#
		And one more, chosen for what its tooltip contains rather than for its
		scores: a site that actually emits a generator tag. Most of this corpus
		does not — a static site usually ships no generator meta at all — so the
		three picked above can easily all be sites where the "Built with" row is
		absent, which makes the examples misrepresent the component.

		Pushing into an array declared outside the loop is the only way to carry a
		value out of one: a `set` inside a loop does not survive it, but the array
		reference does, which is also what makes the length check work as a "first
		match wins" guard.
	#}
	{% set withGenerator = [] %}
	{% for entry in measured %}
		{% if entry.generator and not withGenerator.length and entry.url not in demoUrls %}
			{% set withGenerator = (withGenerator.push(entry), withGenerator) %}
		{% endif %}
	{% endfor %}
	{% if withGenerator.length %}{% set demo = (demo.push(withGenerator[0]), demo) %}{% endif %}

	<div class="embed-demo">
		{% for entry in demo %}
		<div class="embed-row">
			{#
				Prefixed with the plugin's own filter. HtmlBasePlugin rewrites href
				and src, but `speedlify-url` is a custom attribute it knows nothing
				about — left as "/" these would ask the domain root for their data
				and 404 on a project site served from a subdirectory.
			#}
			<speedlify-score speedlify-url="{{ '/' | htmlBaseUrl }}" url="{{ entry.url }}"></speedlify-score>
			<code>{{ entry.displayUrl }}</code>
			{#
				Only where the page said so itself. A site whose mark is presumed from
				a listing has no generator in its data file and no "Built with" row in
				the tooltip, so naming one here would promise something the hover does
				not deliver.
			#}
			{% if entry.generator %}<span class="muted small">Built with {{ entry.generator.name }}</span>{% endif %}
		</div>
		{% endfor %}
	</div>
</section>

<section class="panel">
	<h2>Usage</h2>
	<pre><code>&lt;script type="module" src="{{ meta.url }}/js/speedlify-score.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;!-- Scores for the page it sits on --&gt;
&lt;speedlify-score speedlify-url="{{ meta.url }}/"&gt;&lt;/speedlify-score&gt;

&lt;!-- Or a specific URL --&gt;
&lt;speedlify-score speedlify-url="{{ meta.url }}/" url="https://example.com/"&gt;&lt;/speedlify-score&gt;</code></pre>

	<h3>Attributes</h3>
	<p class="muted small">
		Two, and one of them is optional. There is nothing to configure about the
		output: every instance renders the same six circles — the four Lighthouse
		categories, Core Web Vitals, and axe violations — so a page carrying
		several reads as one table rather than a row of different shapes.
		Everything else is in the tooltip.
	</p>
	<div class="table-scroll">
	<table class="grid">
		<thead>
			<tr><th class="col-site">Attribute</th><th class="col-site">Effect</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td class="col-site"><code>speedlify-url</code></td><td class="col-site">Required. This instance's URL.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="col-site"><code>url</code></td><td class="col-site">Which site to show. Defaults to the current page.</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="panel">
	<h2>How it finds the data</h2>
	<p class="muted small">
		There is no index to download. The component slugifies the URL in the
		browser with the same rules the generator uses and fetches
		<code>/api/site/&lt;slug&gt;.json</code> directly. One request, under a
		kilobyte, for exactly the site being shown.
	</p>
	<p class="muted small">
		Several components on one page share a single fetch per URL. Normalization
		matches the generator's, so <code>https://Example.com</code> and
		<code>https://example.com/</code> resolve to the same record.
	</p>
	<p class="muted small">
		Working out the filename is string manipulation, not cryptography, so the
		component needs no secure context and runs on a page served over plain
		http. If two sites ever claim one slug, both are published under a hash
		instead and read here as unmeasured — a miss rather than another site's
		numbers.
	</p>
	<p class="muted small">
		<a href="/api/site/{{ demo[0].slug }}.json">See {{ demo[0].displayUrl }}'s data file</a>
	</p>
</section>
